    "summary": "Cloth, Yoruba woman’s wrapper of aso oke, composed of 12 strips of machine cotton thread, hand-woven and hand-sewn together along the selvedges. Each strip has the same pattern. The warp is of four white and four brown alternating stripes of cotton. The ground weft is of white cotton, over which are woven patterns in discontinuous supplementary threads of blue cotton. These patterns form blocks: there are four chequerboards and four arrangements of triangles which alternate along each strip. The warp ends have been hemmed by hand stitching.",
